  "notes": [
    "Statewide: one rate everywhere (8 places, including DC). Street ranges: the state publishes address lists (25 states). Map: the address is placed on tax maps (17 states). California: the state's own lookup.",
    "In Alaska, Colorado and Missouri the state's own lookup is run by Avalara, so Avalara is checked against itself there. Leaving out those 18 addresses, OpenTaxRate got 258 of 259 and Avalara 258 of 259.",
    "OpenTaxRate ran with every geocoder it supports. It gave no wrong rates and refused 3 addresses. One is in Park City, Utah, a ZIP with six rates that Utah lists only by ZIP+4; the Postal Service has no ZIP+4 for the address, and the map can't separate those rates. The other two are Missouri addresses its files don't list, whose ZIP+4 the map couldn't confirm. Missouri's own lookup, which the hosted API now uses (STATE_LOOKUPS=MO), answers both correctly, but they aren't counted as correct here, since that lookup is also where Missouri's official rates come from.",
    "Self-hosted geocoders only: 253 of 277 right, 0 wrong, 24 refused. Its 24 refusals are addresses it couldn't place on the map (14) or couldn't split without a ZIP+4 (10). Most were placed by one dataset alone, Pelias's OpenAddresses points, and a lone dataset's point is trusted only inside the typed town's outline, or its ZIP where the town has none; the self-hosted Nominatim found only 36 of the 182 addresses it was asked.",
    "Zip-Tax and APIVerve weren't asked this sample: Zip-Tax's figure is from an earlier sample of 255 addresses, 5 per state, of which it was asked 71, and APIVerve's from a separate random sample of 87 addresses (it takes a ZIP code, not a street address). TaxCloud was asked 67 of the 277.",
    "Geoverio was asked the same 277 officially labeled evalA addresses with full street addresses and default caching. Resolved numeric rates and explicit no-sales-tax zero rates are answers; manual-lookup responses count as refusals. Raw responses are retained; rates were not corrected to match official labels."
  ],
